{"verdict": "accept", "reason": "`password_lock: yes` makes the user module run `usermod -L`, which prefixes the `service` user's hash with '!' and so disables password authentication. In the baseline state the unlocked hash gets locked. In the adversarial state the hash already starts with '!', so the module sees it as locked and leaves it that way. Either way, `service` ends with its password disabled. Running as root on local localhost makes become effectively a no-op."}
